  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "XLubXo0g/UyA6+DNwaUQvn2GsdU=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:55.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## The gap\n\n`ph generate subgraph --name <name>` scaffolds resolvers that call the reactor **directly, with no permission check**. Nothing in the generated code consults the authorization service.\n\nThe leak is twofold: the resolver returns document contents the caller may not be entitled to, and \u2014 because a missing document and a forbidden document are indistinguishable in the response \u2014 it also leaks the *existence* of protected documents.\n\n## What to do instead\n\nUse the enforcement helpers `BaseSubgraph` exposes: `assertCanRead`, `assertCanWrite`, `assertCanExecuteOperation`, `assertCanCreate` (all throw Forbidden), plus the non-throwing `canReadDocument` for filtering lists.\n\nAlways re-fetch through `AuthorizedDocumentHandle.fetchIdentifier` rather than the caller's raw identifier \u2014 permission rows key on canonical ids, so reusing a caller-supplied slug after an assert lets an attacker alias one document's slug onto another.\n\n## Source\n\n`apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/03-BuildingUserExperiences/07-Authorization/05-EnforcingAuthorizationInSubgraphs.md` (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:55.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:55.000Z"
    }
  },
